#N canvas 100 100 780 729 10;
#X obj 40 40 jet_win;
#X msg 40 80 create;
#X msg 130 80 destroy;
#X obj 40 130 jet_head 0;
#X obj 40 165 jet_shader plasma.metal;
#X obj 40 200 jet_uvtransform;
#X obj 40 235 jet_rectangle 3.2 2.4;
#X floatatom 40 290 5 -360 360 0 - - -;
#X text 40 315 rotate;
#X floatatom 145 290 5 0.1 4 0 - - -;
#X text 145 315 scale;
#X floatatom 250 290 5 0 3 0 - - -;
#X text 250 315 wrap 0-3;
#X msg 40 355 0.2 0.1;
#X msg 40 383 0 0;
#X msg 40 411 translate -0.3 0;
#X text 40 449 [jet_uvtransform] moves \, turns and scales the PICTURE itself (the texture) \, not a shape - unlike jet_translateXYZ and friends. translate (left list inlet or "translate 0.2 0.1") shifts it in picture widths \, x right and y up. rotate is in degrees around the centre \, scale 2 makes it twice as big (one number scales both axes \, two numbers x and y). wrap decides what shows outside the picture: 0 edge colour \, 1 repeat (try scale 0.4) \, 2 mirror \, 3 black.;
#X text 40 569 Creation arguments fill the parameters in order: [jet_uvtransform 0 0 30 1.5]. Like every jet effect it takes the chain's current texture and hands the result on as the new current texture \; the output has the size of the input (send "resolution w h" to change it). The parameter inlets only store - the next bang from the chain uses them - and the same values can be sent to the left inlet by name \, "info" lists them. After the chain below it has run \, the block puts the texture back the way it found it \, so it acts like a separator for textures.;
#X connect 1 0 0 0;
#X connect 2 0 0 0;
#X connect 3 0 4 0;
#X connect 4 0 5 0;
#X connect 5 0 6 0;
#X connect 7 0 5 2;
#X connect 9 0 5 3;
#X connect 11 0 5 4;
#X connect 13 0 5 1;
#X connect 14 0 5 1;
#X connect 15 0 5 0;
